const probCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Uncertainty has structure', note: 'Single events are unpredictable; ensembles are not. The insurer cannot know who crashes — only how many, on average.' },
    { math: '2 · Model frequency and severity', note: 'Two random pieces: how many claims (frequency) and how large each is (severity). Total cost is their combination.' },
    { math: '3 · Expectation sets the price floor', note: 'E[total] = n·p·E[claim]. Price below expected cost and ruin is arithmetic, not bad luck.' },
    { math: '4 · Variance sets the capital', note: 'Even a fair price fluctuates. The spread of outcomes (std dev) tells the insurer how much capital absorbs a bad year.' },
    { math: '5 · The law of large numbers is the business', note: 'Pooling more policies shrinks relative variance — scale itself is the oldest risk-reduction technology.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/actuarial-science/foundational/probability-for-actuaries">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Insurance rests on a strange and wonderful fact: nobody can predict whether a specific
    house will burn this year, yet an insurer holding a hundred thousand houses can state
    its total fire losses for next year within a few percent. Probability is the mathematics
    of that forecastability, and actuarial science is probability applied to money with
    dates attached. Two quantities run the whole business: the <em>expectation</em> of
    losses, which sets the price floor, and the <em>variance</em>, which sets the capital
    that absorbs the bad years. Every other actuarial idea is elaboration on that pair.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founding exam of the profession (the Society of Actuaries' Exam P) is essentially
    the toolkit, in three shelves: general probability and combinatorics (how to reason
    about conditions and count possibilities), random variables and distributions (the
    catalogue of standard shapes of uncertainty), and moments — expectation, variance and
    their kin (the summary numbers that make pricing and capital possible). This chapter
    walks the shelves in order, but with the actuary's eye: each tool is introduced by the
    money question it answers.
  </p>
  <p>
    One warning belongs up front. Exam-style probability rewards a specific habit: never
    compute with a story when a definition will do. Events are sets; probabilities are
    measures on them; conditions shrink the measure. Almost every famous probability error,
    in courtrooms and casinos alike, is a definition quietly replaced by a plausible
    narrative.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from uncertainty to a defensible price" widget="equationStep" config={probCfg} />

  <h2>General probability and combinatorics</h2>
  <p>
    The indispensable tool is <em>conditional probability</em> — how a probability changes
    when information arrives — and its inversion formula, <em>Bayes' theorem</em>, which
    converts "the probability of evidence given the cause" into "the probability of the
    cause given the evidence." The classic worked example shows why the inversion is not
    optional. A fraud-screening model flags 99 percent of fraudulent claims (sensitivity)
    and wrongly flags 5 percent of honest ones. If 1 percent of claims are fraudulent, what
    is the chance that a flagged claim actually is fraudulent? Out of 10,000 claims: 100
    are fraudulent and 99 get flagged; 9,900 are honest and 495 get flagged. So a flag
    means fraud with probability 99 ÷ (99 + 495) ≈ 16.7 percent. Nearly five out of six
    flags are false — not because the model is bad, but because the base rate is small.
    Actuaries meet this daily in fraud triage, in diagnostic-style underwriting evidence,
    and in any "the test is 99 percent accurate" headline.
  </p>
  <p>
    Combinatorics is the counting engine beneath every probability, and two patterns cover
    most ground. <em>Combinations</em> (order does not matter) count selections: choosing 3
    of 20 claims at random for audit gives C(20,3) = 1,140 distinct audit sets — the
    denominator when you want the probability the sample contains both suspect claims.
    <em>The birthday-style surprise</em> shows why naive multiplication misleads: in a
    23-person office, the chance that two share a birthday exceeds 50 percent, because the
    question is really about 253 pairs, not 23 people. Insurance books are full of such
    pairings — correlated policies, common exposures — and the counting instinct (what is
    the population of ways this can go wrong?) is what separates sound aggregate modelling
    from wishful addition.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Independence</em> is the property that lets probabilities multiply, and knowing
    when it holds is half the practice. Two events are independent exactly when learning
    one does not change the other's probability — P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) — and most
    actuarial modelling is an argument about whether that equation applies. Separate
    drivers in separate towns: plausibly yes. Fire losses across flats in one building:
    absolutely not — one fire takes the whole floor. The multiplication rule builds big
    models from small ones; its misuse builds big mistakes from small assumptions, which
    is why every independence claim in a model deserves the question a regulator would
    ask: what happens in the year the equation fails?
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> P(A given B) and P(B given A) are interchangeable. They
    are not, and confusing them is the <em>prosecutor's fallacy</em>: "the match is
    one-in-a-million, so the chance this defendant is innocent is one-in-a-million" ignores
    that a million-odd people also match. In the fraud example: P(fraud given flag) ≈ 17
    percent while P(flag given fraud) = 99 percent — the same evidence, two very different
    probabilities, and the whole of Bayesian practice lives in the space between.
  </p>

  <h2>Random variables and distributions</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>random variable</em> is a quantity whose value is uncertain — the number of claims
    next quarter, the size of the next claim — and a <em>distribution</em> is its shape:
    which values occur with what probabilities. The exam's famous zoo is really a catalogue
    of behaviours, each tied to a situation. The <em>binomial</em> counts successes in n
    independent trials with probability p each (how many of 2,000 policies claim); its mean
    is np and its variance np(1 − p). The <em>Poisson</em> counts rare events in time or
    space (claims per week, hurricanes per season); it has one parameter, and its mean and
    variance are both λ — the signature of "events at a steady rate, independently." The
    <em>exponential</em> models waiting times between such events, and is
    <em>memoryless</em>: having waited ten minutes tells you nothing about the next ten —
    the mathematically pure form of "the light never changes faster for having waited."
    The <em>normal</em> is the shape that emerges when many small independent effects sum
    (the central limit theorem, below), and the heavy-tailed families — lognormal, Pareto —
    model claim <em>severities</em>, where a fire that totals a factory must be possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    The frequency–severity split is the actuary's first modelling decision: total loss =
    (number of claims) × (sizes of claims), and the two random pieces behave differently —
    counts are often Poisson-ish and well behaved, while severities carry the heavy tails.
    Choosing them separately, then combining, is what makes the arithmetic of a whole
    insurance book tractable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Distributions are read through a small set of standard lenses worth naming once. The
    <em>cumulative distribution function</em> F(x) answers "what is the probability of
    being at or under x?" and is the universal translator between continuous densities and
    actual numbers — a pricing question like "what loss level will one-year-in-twenty
    exceed?" is asking F's inverse (its 95th percentile). <em>Discrete</em> variables take
    separated values and are described by point probabilities; <em>continuous</em> ones
    take ranges and are described by densities, where "the probability of exactly 2.5" is
    zero and only intervals carry mass. Transformations matter too: an insurer's deductible
    turns every claim size X into a payment of (X − d) when X is large enough — a random
    variable built from a random variable, and the ordinary machinery of expectation and
    variance handles the conversion without ceremony.
  </p>

  <h2>Expectation, variance and moments</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Expectation</em> is the long-run average: the probability-weighted centre of the
    distribution, and the only defensible starting point for a price. Its superpower is
    <em>linearity</em> — the expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations, no
    independence required — which is why an insurer can price a million heterogeneous
    policies by summing expected costs. <em>Variance</em> (the average squared spread
    around the mean) prices the uncertainty; its square root, the standard deviation, is
    the "typical miss." Higher <em>moments</em> refine the portrait: skewness (do losses
    lean right?) and kurtosis (how fat is the tail?), which is why they are called
    moments — successive integrals of powers, like moments of mass in mechanics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run the pool arithmetic with the widget's defaults: 2,000 policies, each with a 5
    percent chance of a 20,000-dollar claim. Expected total claims: 2,000 × 0.05 × 20,000
    = 2,000,000 dollars — the fair premium is 1,000 dollars per policy before expenses and
    profit loading. But the total is random: its standard deviation is
    √(n·p·(1 − p)) × severity = √95 × 20,000 ≈ 195,000 dollars, a coefficient of variation
    near 10 percent. Quadruple the book to 8,000 policies and the expected loss quadruples,
    but the standard deviation only doubles (it grows as √n), so the relative volatility
    <em>halves</em>. That 1-over-√n shrinkage is the law of large numbers wearing a
    business suit: scale itself is the oldest risk-reduction technology, and it is why
    pooling — not prediction — is insurance's core competence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="expected cost, loading, and volatility of the pool" widget="sliderLab" config={premCfg} />

  <p>
    The <em>central limit theorem</em> then ties the shelves together: sums of many
    independent contributions — like a large pool of mostly claim-free policies — are
    approximately normal regardless of the individual shapes, which is why the normal
    distribution haunts aggregate pricing and why "within two standard deviations" is
    meaningful shorthand even when nobody's individual loss is remotely bell-shaped.
  </p>
  <p>
    Skewness earns a final, practical note: in heavy-tailed loss distributions the
    <em>mean sits above the median</em>, often by a lot. The typical claim is small — most
    fender-benders, most power-outages — but the premium must be set by the mean, because
    the rare enormous claims really arrive and really must be paid. This is why
    "average claim" in a press release and "expected loss" in a rate filing are different
    numbers with different jobs, and why reinsurance exists: the tail above some threshold
    is the part of the distribution worth selling to someone whose balance sheet is sized
    for it. Price the mean, capitalise the tail, and never confuse the two — that is the
    whole profession in one sentence.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The exam toolkit has deeper rooms. <em>Moment-generating functions</em> encode all the
    moments of a distribution in one object, and their payoffs are practical: sums of
    independent variables get MGFs that multiply (making aggregate loss distributions
    computable), and an MGF, when it exists, identifies the distribution completely — the
    rigorous version of "this looks normal." <em>Joint distributions and covariance</em>
    quantify how variables move together, and they sharpen the limits of pooling: the
    1-over-√n smoothness assumes independence, and the assumptions fail spectacularly when
    policies share a perils — hurricanes, wildfires, cyber events hit thousands of policies
    at once, converting "diversified" books into single bets. This is why catastrophe
    insurance is reinsurance-heavy: correlation, not frequency, is what actually breaks
    insurers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later theory builds upward from the same base. <em>Ruin theory</em> asks the founding
    question directly — with premium income and random claims, what is the probability the
    fund ever falls below zero? — and answers it with the adjustment coefficient and
    Cramér–Lundberg bounds, turning "hold enough capital" into a formula. <em>Credibility
    theory</em> blends a group's own experience with the class average, weighting by how
    much data deserves how much trust — Bayes made actuarial. Beyond Exam P, the syllabi
    add time: Exam FM's interest theory prices money across dates (discounting, annuities,
    bonds), and life contingencies fuses mortality tables with discount factors into the
    valuation of pensions and life products. The open frontier is statistical:
    heavier-than-Pareto tails in a warming climate, correlated systemic risks, and machine-
    learned pricing that regulators now police for bias — the same two questions, price and
    capital, in an era with sharper tools and sharper edges.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SOA Exam P/FM syllabi; Ross, <em>A First Course in Probability</em>; Klugman, Panjer &amp; Willmot, <em>Loss Models</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
